Day 5: Online Machine Learning

If you have heard that the more you use a product the better it gets, that is often online ML.

Online learning is done incrementally.
We feed small batches to the model.
Every small batch makes the ML model smarter.
Because of this we can train on the server.

Small amount of data -> ML -> test -> server.
The server gets new data every time.
We train the ML model on the server.

Examples: chatbots on devices like Alexa and Google. They read chat data and train on the server.
If we use an online keyboard, the more we use it the more personalized it gets. YouTube gives real time suggestions.

Online learning is popular nowadays.

When to use batch learning and online learning

The main idea is concept drift: when the nature or context changes rapidly, use online learning. Examples: stock, sales, ecommerce.

Use online learning to reduce cost in some setups.

Online learning is very fast. It helps make the system fast.

We can use scikit learn or dedicated libraries like River, or Vowpal Wabbit.

Scikit learn supports batch learning more as the default.

Learning rate: how often we update the model with new data. We need to pick the best learning rate. This is one of the hardest parts of online learning.

Out of core learning: when the data is so huge we cannot train it all at once.
In this case we use online learning and give the system small chunks of data.

Big data -> split to small data -> model -> test -> server.

Disadvantages of online learning:
It is tricky to use. It is easy to start training but risky.
If the server is hacked or we get spam data, someone could control the incoming data. That is risky.
We need a monitoring system to detect bad data.
If we get hacked or bad data arrives, we should be able to roll back and pause online learning.

Online vs offline

Offline is not complex.
Online is complex.

Computation power: batch training often uses strong local machines. Online needs strong server computation too.

In production, batch is easier and online is harder.

In application:
If the data pattern is mostly constant, batch learning can work.
If the pattern changes fast, online learning fits better.

Tools:
Batch learning: Spark, TensorFlow, PyTorch.

Online learning: Storm, MOA, scikit-multiflow, and similar tools.
